WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.066
CodingBooth provides isolated, repeatable, and shareable development environments designed to eliminate the classic "Works on My Machine" problem once and for all.

00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:14.485
This friction occurs when software runs perfectly for the developer who wrote it, but fails inconsistently on another machine.

00:00:14.485 --> 00:00:18.918
It is a problem driven by environmental drift, usually caused by these hidden culprits:

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:22.663
Failed Experiments or Troubleshooting steps that were never reversed.

00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:25.784
Cross-Project Pollution bleeding over from other projects.

00:00:25.784 --> 00:00:29.769
Evolutionary Drift as projects evolve but unequally on different machines.

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:34.138
AI-driven noise - quick fixes left behind as untracked, undocumented mess.

00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:37.307
Quick fixes that make it work but were never documented.

00:00:37.307 --> 00:00:40.524
When the environment is broken, the consequences can be expensive.

00:00:40.524 --> 00:00:45.710
Apps fail sometimes elsewhere but you can't debug it because it works perfectly on yours.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:54.160
Every time a new developer joins, hours, even days are lost manually installing tools, fighting version conflicts, and debugging environment issues.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.930
Your tests become flaky — passing on one run, failing on another — leaving you not quite sure if your code is actually working.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.219
Then comes the worst part: unexpected failures in production.

00:01:04.219 --> 00:01:10.149
Subtle differences might make the app work at first but fail some friday evening when hiting a rare condition.

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:13.700
All of this slowly destroys trust in the development process.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.854
You start second-guessing everything.

00:01:15.854 --> 00:01:20.860
You run more tests hoping that more coverage should help but the build runs for hours.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.024
Progress slows down.

00:01:22.024 --> 00:01:23.305
Confidence evaporates.

00:01:23.305 --> 00:01:27.338
This is the hidden cost every team pays for 'Works on My Machine'."

00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:28.730
Coding Booth changes that.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:33.052
It uses containers to isolate the development environment from the host.

00:01:33.052 --> 00:01:36.797
Booth config lives with the code so all team members get the same setting.

00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:39.883
The environment is re-contructed fresh from the config.

00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:41.454
No place for drifts to hide.

00:01:41.454 --> 00:01:45.391
Booth handles the host-container user by matching the user ID and host ID.

00:01:45.391 --> 00:01:52.929
It provides different user interfaces ranging from terminal to a full Linux desktop to fit your project's needs or your preferences.

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:54.082
With CodingBooth, ....

00:01:54.082 --> 00:01:57.038
Onboarding a new member can be done with minimal efforts.

00:01:57.038 --> 00:02:00.564
They can start learning the code and even making changes in minutes.

00:02:00.564 --> 00:02:05.653
You can be confident that if you can development on one machine, the same thing can be done on another.

00:02:05.653 --> 00:02:10.407
With the development done in the container, your code can stay clean -- no stains or scars.

00:02:10.407 --> 00:02:16.456
Proper version pinning can be done so the dev environment can be usable even after untouched for months or years.

00:02:16.456 --> 00:02:22.602
"Booth config" simplies the development environement setup so most tools only need one line of config.

00:02:22.602 --> 00:02:25.972
All in all, CodingBooth helps with the development environment.

00:02:25.972 --> 00:02:28.700
Put CodingBooth in your project and Thank me later.

